\details{
Noise is rendered as varying opacity of a single \code{color}, from fully
transparent at the noise field's minimum to \code{alpha} at its maximum --
a mottled, cloud-like texture rather than a hard-edged one.

A raster tile has no baked-in direction the way a hatch line does, so
\code{\link[=fill_hatch]{fill_hatch()}}'s tile-edge "dashing" problem doesn't apply directly, but
an \emph{ordinary} noise field still isn't periodic, and \code{\link[grid:pattern]{grid::pattern()}}'s
\code{extend = "repeat"} will visibly seam wherever one tile edge fails to
match the next. \code{fill_noise()} avoids this by sampling the noise on a
torus: each raster pixel's \verb{(u, v)} tile coordinate is mapped onto a pair
of circles (\code{ambient::gen_simplex()}'s 4 dimensions, \code{x}/\code{y} for \code{u} and
\code{z}/\code{t} for \code{v}) rather than sampled directly, so the field is
mathematically periodic in both directions and tiles with no visible
seam, at the cost of the noise "wrapping around" within each tile rather
than varying smoothly across a larger area.

In practice, a very faint seam can still be visible at tile boundaries on
some devices, even though the underlying field is exactly periodic; this
appears to be an artifact of how the graphics device samples a repeated
raster tile (it persists regardless of \code{interpolate} and doesn't improve
with higher \code{resolution}), not a flaw in the noise field itself, and is
far subtler than the tile-edge mismatch \code{\link[=fill_hatch]{fill_hatch()}} has to actively
avoid.
